course: euphemisms
lesson: 39
title: Soft words for hard things: rudeness and swearing
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Newspapers, teachers and polite grown-ups often have to report swearing without repeating it. So they use cover words: "strong language", "colourful language", "some choice words", "expletive deleted".
explain: When you read "the coach used colourful language", nobody painted anything: the coach swore. The word "colourful" makes the swearing sound almost cheerful.
explain: People also apologise for their own swearing with a joke: "Pardon my French!" pretends the rude word was a foreign one.
explain: And there are the softened swear words themselves: "ruddy", "bloomin'" and "flaming" for "bloody", "feck" in Ireland for the f-word, and "fiddlesticks" or "bother" from grannies who never swear at all.
fact: "An f-bomb" means one use of the f-word, the strongest common swear word; it is American slang, as in "he dropped an f-bomb".
fact: "Feck" is an Irish word, milder than the f-word, used in anger or as a joke.
fact: "Fiddlesticks!" is an old-fashioned mild word for annoyance instead of a real swear.
fact: "Bother!" is an old-fashioned British mild word for annoyance, used instead of "damn".
fact: "Shucks!" is an old-fashioned American mild word for disappointment or shyness, used instead of a rude word.
fact: "Bloomin'" is a British mild stand-in for the swear word "bloody", as in "bloomin' rain".
fact: "Ruddy" is an old-fashioned British stand-in for "bloody".
fact: "Flaming" is a British stand-in for "bloody", as in "the flaming bus is late"; nothing is on fire.
fact: "Bleeping" stands for any swear word, as if the word had been covered by a beep on TV.
fact: "Expletive deleted" means a swear word was said here and has been left out of the written record.
fact: "A four-letter word" means a swear word, because many English swear words have four letters.
fact: "Pardon my French" means "excuse my swearing"; the rude word was English, not French.
fact: "Excuse my language" is a polite way of saying "sorry for swearing".
fact: "Colourful language" means swearing; it is a British polite phrase.
fact: "Strong language" means swearing; TV and film warnings use it.
fact: "Turned the air blue" means swore a lot; it is a British joke.
fact: "Effing and blinding" means swearing a lot; it is British slang.
fact: "Some choice words" means swear words or insults, as in "she had some choice words for the referee".
fact: "Told someone where to go" means told someone very rudely to go away.
fact: "Naff off!" is a British slang way to say "go away", softer than the f-word version.
exercise: An American news story says a singer "dropped an f-bomb on live TV". What happened? | The singer said the f-word | The singer threw a firework | The singer fell off the stage | The singer forgot a song
exercise: An Irish uncle says "Ah, feck it!" What is "feck"? | A milder Irish stand-in for the f-word | An Irish word for a fish | A word for a type of hat | An Irish word for "thank you"
exercise: Great-aunt May spills her tea and says "Fiddlesticks!" What does she mean? | She is annoyed | She wants to play the violin | She needs more sticks | She is happy with the tea
exercise: What does "Oh, bother!" usually mean? | A mild cry of annoyance | Please disturb me | I have a brother | I want to help you
exercise: What does an American cowboy mean by "Aw, shucks"? | Mild disappointment or shyness | He is shelling corn | He wants new shoes | He is very angry
exercise: In "this bloomin' rain", what is "bloomin'"? | A mild stand-in for "bloody" | A word about flowers opening | A word meaning very light | A word for spring
exercise: What is "ruddy" in "the ruddy car won't start"? | An old-fashioned stand-in for "bloody" | A word meaning red, like a healthy face in the cold | A word meaning rusty | A kind of car part
exercise: "The flaming train is late again!" Is the train on fire? | No, "flaming" stands in for "bloody" | Yes, the engine is burning | Yes, it is a steam train | No, "flaming" means very fast
exercise: What does "bleeping" stand for in "turn that bleeping music down"? | Any swear word, as if beeped out | An alarm that beeps when there is smoke in the house | The sound of a phone | Music made by robots
exercise: A printed record of a speech shows "[expletive deleted]". What does it mean? | A swear word was said and left out | A word was spelt wrongly | The speaker coughed | A secret was kept out of the printed record
exercise: What does "she used a four-letter word" mean? | She swore at someone | She spelled a short word | She said "love" | She wrote a short poem
exercise: A man swears, then says "Pardon my French." What does he mean? | Excuse my swearing | Excuse my accent | I speak French badly | I am from France
exercise: What does "excuse my language" mean? | Sorry for swearing | Sorry I speak another language | Sorry I talk too fast | Sorry for my bad grammar
exercise: A British teacher says the plumber used "colourful language". What did he do? | He swore a lot | He talked about paint | He spoke very kindly | He told funny stories
exercise: A film poster says "Contains strong language". What does it warn about? | Swearing | Very loud sound | Hard words | Shouting only
exercise: "When he saw the bill, he turned the air blue." What did he do? | He swore a lot | He fainted | He opened a window | He went pale
exercise: What does "effing and blinding" mean? | Swearing a lot | Going blind | Eating fast | Saying F and B letters
exercise: "She had some choice words for the referee." What did she say? | Swear words or insults | Kind words of thanks | Choices of team players | Advice on the rules
exercise: "When the salesman pushed again, Gran told him where to go." What did Gran do? | Told him very rudely to go away | Gave him directions to the shop | Showed him the way home | Told him the bus times
exercise: A British teen says "Naff off!" What does she want? | The other person to go away | The other person to take a nap | The other person to be quiet | The other person to take off a coat
test: Newspaper: "The manager's post-match comments cannot be printed in a family newspaper." What does this suggest? | He swore a lot | He spoke French | He said nothing at all | He praised the players
test: TV guide: "Warning: contains strong language from the start." What should a parent expect? | Swearing | Long words | Shouting matches only | A foreign language
test: "Grandad hit his thumb with the hammer and turned the air blue." What happened next? | He swore a lot | He went cold | He painted the room | He had a nosebleed
test: Mia reckons "pardon my French" means the person is actually speaking French. right? | No, it means sorry for swearing | Yes, they switched to French | Yes, it is a French greeting | No, it means "I'm learning French"
test: "ruddy cat's been at the bin again!" Is the speaker saying the cat is red? | No, "ruddy" is a soft swear for "bloody" | Yes, the cat has ginger fur all over its back | Yes, the cat has a red face | No, the cat's name is Ruddy
test: "he dropped like 3 f-bombs in one sentence lol" What did he do? | He said the f-word three times | He threw three water bombs | He fell over three times | He scored three goals
test: White House tape transcript: "I don't give a [expletive deleted]." What was left out? | A swear word | A name | A number | A secret plan
test: A mum, cheerful: "Oh fiddlesticks, I've forgotten the milk." How upset is she? | Only mildly annoyed | Furious and swearing | Very sad and crying | Frightened and panicking
test: An Irish girl laughs, "Feck off, you're joking!" What does she mean? | She is surprised and doesn't believe it, in fun | She is angry and wants a fight | She wants the other girl to leave forever | She is asking for help
test: "The neighbours were effing and blinding at each other till midnight." What was going on? | A loud argument full of swearing | A quiet game of cards | A party with loud music and dancing | An eye test
test: "excuse my language but that bloomin' dog — sorry, that lovely dog — ate my shoe." What does the speaker really feel? | Annoyed with the dog | Proud of the dog | Scared of the dog | Happy about the shoe
test: Which of these is a phrase for someone else's swearing, not a soft swear word itself? | Some choice words | Fiddlesticks | Bother | Shucks
exam: Where a published report records "expletive deleted", determine what has been omitted from the record. | A vulgar or profane word | A personal name | A confidential figure | A grammatical error
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the warning "contains strong language". | The material includes swearing | The material employs difficult vocabulary | The material is delivered at high volume | The material is in a foreign tongue
exam: Ascertain the function of the expression "pardon my French" when appended to an utterance. | An apology for profanity | An apology for a foreign accent | A request for translation | A declaration of nationality
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the term "a four-letter word". | It denotes a swear word | It denotes any word of four letters | It denotes a term of affection | It denotes an abbreviation
